# Generated, not authored. The canonical reference is a pure function of the seed
# and the code, so any run regenerates it in a few milliseconds -- and a copy left
# over from an older build is worse than none, because nothing checks that a
# stored reference still matches the code that reads it. It would simply be
# believed, and healthy replicas would be refused for disagreeing with it.
